Z||000007||lawrence||06-21-2001||12:35 PM||||I sell a Daytime Running Lights (DRL) module that does this.  Comes will all the parts you need and wiring instructions.  If you are interested, click the link below for more info.  The DRL page also has a link to the installation instructions.<P>Have fun,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/products" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/products</A>  - WebElectric Products||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||06-21-2001||08:38 PM||||Lawrence's module will work with OE parking lights. If you want to use a driving light in true DRL mode (Wich is dimmer than full on. It makes the bulbs last longer.) then you need either GM's DRL module (Ignore them when they say it wont work with fiero... you aren't using the main headlights.) or and after market module.<P>If you get one from GM you want the one Without "twilite sentinal." The part # I have is 12370131.||||reg||1
Z||000009||lawrence||06-22-2001||12:19 PM||||Ogre,<P>This is not exactly true.  There are two types of DRLs currently in use.  The one you speak of controls the high beams, and you are correct, they run at about 7 volts.  You see these on Saturns and some GM SUVs, and they won't work on Fieros unless you want to drive with the headlights up.  I don't care for this type of DRL because even though they aren't at full brightness, they are aimed high and cause a lot of glare (at least they do for me).<P>However, on other cars, like my Regal, other GM mid-sized and some Cryslers, the turn signal filament is turned on at full brightness (not the parking light filament).  When you turn on your lights, the DRLs turn off and operate as normal turn signals.<P>This is how my modules work.<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/products" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/products</A>  - WebElectric Products||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||06-22-2001||09:39 PM||||Lawrence, I meant that the GM DRL module would be used to run aftermarket driving lights and not the OE headlights. You right, it won't work with the OE headlights. (If I ever find aftermarket lights I like,,, I'll hook them up to the GM DRL unit.)<P>I'm not a huge fan of OE DRL installs. I do think the module would work well with after market driving lights that tend to have a more narrow beam and be mounted lower.<P>Sorry I messed up describing your module. I've read that article several times and still messed up describing it. Silly Ogre. Bonk. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-22-2001).]||||reg||1
